---
title: Capabilities and execution modes
description: Request only the app services you use and understand which runner can execute them.
sidebar:
  order: 8
---

A **capability** is a permission your app requests in lui.json. A **service** is
the API you call. Their names differ: HttpService needs `network.client`, not a
token named HttpService.

The **host** runs your code. For ordinary SDK work, that is the supplied native
runner; you do not need to implement a native provider to use a service the SDK
already supplies. Custom embedders install providers when building their own
host. A manifest cannot add a provider that the selected platform does not have.

## Add a capability

For clipboard use, replace your existing Capabilities array with:

```json
"Capabilities": ["Clipboard"]
```

This is one **field fragment** inside lui.json, not a complete manifest. Preserve
the other fields and any other capabilities your app still needs. Then use
Run App; Inspect Layout deliberately refuses privileged apps.

| I need | Capability and setup |
| --- | --- |
| Make an HTTP/HTTPS request | `network.client`; use HttpService or JsonClient. |
| Host an HTTP endpoint | `network.server`; use HttpServerService. |
| Raw TCP or UDP | `network.raw` plus the applicable client/server direction grant. |
| Plain-text clipboard | `Clipboard` and the supported desktop adapter. |
| Choose a file path | `Dialogs` and a supported picker. The returned path is not unrestricted file-reading permission. |
| App-private preferences/storage | `Storage` plus `Storage.ApplicationId` in preview.2. See [Preferences](../../libraries/luiworkbench/preferences/). |
| Desktop notifications or tray icons | `Notifications` or `Tray`, respectively; the selected Windows shell supports them, GTK reports unsupported. |

Tokens are case-sensitive. HTTP does not need network.raw. PlatformService,
ThemeService, PerformanceService, WindowService and declared-asset lookup do not
need an additional capability token.

## Keep a local example on this machine

For an app that should contact/listen only on this computer, add this top-level
field to lui.json. It narrows existing network grants; it does not grant them.
Supply all six fields with the current SDK project runner. Zero port limits
allow any port, including a free port requested with `Port = 0`.

```json
"NetworkPolicy": {
  "ClientLoopbackOnly": true,
  "ServerLoopbackOnly": true,
  "ClientPortMin": 0,
  "ClientPortMax": 0,
  "ServerPortMin": 0,
  "ServerPortMax": 0
}
```

This is a field fragment inside the manifest. Loopback means the same computer;
another phone or PC cannot reach a loopback-only server. Use the complete
local HTTP recipe below to try client/server behavior without a fixed port.

## Check, run and inspect do different jobs

| Tool | Runs app code? | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Check Types | No | Find source/type errors in declared Development files. It cannot prove that a host grant or endpoint is available. |
| Run App | Yes | Try the native application with supported grants and providers. |
| Inspect Layout | Yes, as a separate unprivileged copy | Inspect the shared Instance tree and layout. It does not attach to the running native window and refuses privileged capabilities/native extensions. |

Restricted static checking is not supported. Do not remove a needed capability
just to make Inspect Layout accept an app; use Run App for that behavior.

## When the app will not start

Save files, check types, then read **LUI Preview** in VS Code’s Output panel.
Confirm the exact token, matching SDK pin and supported host. A callback failure
can leave the window open while the operation fails, so a visible window alone
does not establish success.

For HTTP, distinguish a denied grant, a refused host address policy, an endpoint
that is not running, and an HTTP error status. Start with the
[complete local HTTP recipe](../recipes/#local-http-client-and-server), which
includes both sides and keeps traffic on loopback. Do not add every capability
as a recovery step.

## Custom hosts and native extensions — advanced

`HostServices` is for explicitly registered custom providers; their separate
grants still apply. `NativeExtensions` is privileged native loading. These are
host/framework integration topics, not requirements for an ordinary window,
button, HTTP request or preference store.

Native extensions require an explicitly approved host launch with `--allow-native`.
Editor Run App does not request that approval. The
[native-service design](https://github.com/gmoddev/LUI/blob/sdk-v0.1.0-preview.2/docs/NATIVE-SERVICES.md)
describes provider implementation and lifetime for people extending/embedding
LUI. Application code should keep using the public Luau interfaces.
